EXT. SELF-STORAGE FACILITY ROOFTOP - NIGHT

A flat tar roof ringed by rows of orange doors. A city glows beyond the chain-link. Two lawn chairs, a cooler, a portable speaker playing something low. A tangle of fairy lights strung across a vent pipe, half of them dead.

CASSIDY ODEKUNLE, seventeen, slouches in one chair in a thrift-store prom dress and sneakers, her knees drawn up. Beside her, ELIJAH "ELI" BRANDVOLD, seventeen, sits in a rented tux with the bow tie loose, a paper cup of lukewarm soda balanced on his knee.

CASSIDY
You didn't have to come up here.

ELI
You texted me the address.

CASSIDY
As a joke.

ELI
It didn't read like a joke.

CASSIDY
Everything reads like a joke when you're me.

He sets the cup down on the tar. He leans back and looks at the sky, which has only three stars in it.

ELI
You missed your own prom.

CASSIDY
I didn't miss it. I left it.

ELI
Why?

CASSIDY
Because Kayleigh Tran said my dress looked like a curtain.

ELI
It does look like a curtain.

CASSIDY
I know it looks like a curtain! It's my grandmother's curtain!

She laughs, startled, and then her face crumples. She pulls the dress's hem over her knees and presses her forehead to it.

ELI
Hey.

CASSIDY
Don't.

ELI
Cass.

CASSIDY
I said don't.

He sits very still. The speaker's song ends. In the quiet, a train passes below, a long iron murmur. She wipes her face with her wrist.

CASSIDY (CONT'D)
My mom doesn't know. She thinks I'm at the after-party.

ELI
Is there an after-party?

CASSIDY
There's always an after-party.

ELI
I'll take you.

CASSIDY
I don't want to go.

ELI
Then I'll stay.

CASSIDY
You hate roofs.

ELI
I hate heights. Roofs are just heights with furniture.

She laughs, a small wet laugh. He holds out the cup of soda. She drinks, makes a face, and gives it back.

CASSIDY
Warm.

ELI
Yeah.

They watch the city. Somewhere far off, a siren starts and fades.

CASSIDY
Eli?

ELI
Yeah?

CASSIDY
Do you ever think everyone is faking?

ELI
All the time.

CASSIDY
Even Kayleigh?

ELI
Especially Kayleigh.

She smiles. She slides her hand across the armrest and he takes it without looking, and they sit like that, two people on a roof among a hundred locked doors.

Then Eli's phone buzzes on the tar. They both glance at it. The screen lights: MOM — 14 MISSED CALLS.

ELI ^
Oh no.

CASSIDY ^
Oh no.

He grabs it. She snatches it from him and holds it up out of reach, and he lunges, and the chairs scrape, and the cooler topples, and ice spills across the roof in a bright white rush.

ELI (CONT'D)
Give it!

CASSIDY
Not until you say it!

ELI
Say what?

CASSIDY
That you're glad you came.

He stops. Ice crunches under his shoes. He looks at her, tux askew, face flushed, hair full of fairy-light glare.

ELI
I'm glad I came.

CASSIDY
Good.

She hands him the phone. He doesn't look at it. He puts it face-down on the tar.

ELI
You owe me a dance.

CASSIDY
There's no music.

ELI
There's the speaker.

She reaches over and taps it. A scratchy waltz starts up. She stands, awkward in her sneakers, and offers him her hand.

CASSIDY
Don't step on my curtain.

ELI
I'll try.
